{"name":"Ginger Ale Fizz","hex":"#f5dfbc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ginger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The pungent aromatic rhizome of a tropical Asian herb, Zingiber officinale, used as a spice and as a stimulant and acarminative.","The plant that produces this rhizome."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a reddish-brown color.","Having hair or fur of this color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginger"},{"term":"ale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Aleut."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A beer or beerlike drink produced by so-called warm fermentation and unpressurized.","A beer or beerlike drink made without hops."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ale"},{"term":"fizz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An emission of a rapid stream of bubbles.","The sound of such an emission."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To emit bubbles.","To make a rapid hissing or bubbling sound."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fizz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ginger ale","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger ale is a carbonated soft drink flavoured with ginger. It is consumed on its own or used as a mixer, often with spirit-based drinks. There are two main types of ginger ale. The golden style is credited to the Irish doctor Thomas Joseph Cantrell. The dry style, a paler drink with a much milder ginger flavour, was created by Canadian John McLaughlin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Vernors_ginger_ale.jpg/330px-Vernors_ginger_ale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger_ale","wikidata":"Q1162763"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger","wikidata":"Q35625"},{"title":"Ale","blocks":[{"text":"Ale is a style of beer, brewed using a warm fermentation method. In medieval England, the term referred to a drink brewed without hops."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Real_Ale_2004-05-09_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ale","wikidata":"Q208385"},{"title":"Effervescence","blocks":[{"text":"Effervescence is the escape of gas from an aqueous solution and the foaming or fizzing that results from that release. The word effervescence is derived from the Latin verb fervere, preceded by the prefix ex. It has the same linguistic root as the word fermentation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Soda_bubbles_macro.jpg/330px-Soda_bubbles_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effervescence","wikidata":"Q2797526"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg/500px-Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg/500px-Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1916 Toronto Star ad for Canada Dry ginger ale","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/